WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:15.000
All right, so today we're going to continue our discussion about motion planning by discussing

00:15.000 --> 00:20.200
another class of algorithms referred to as sampling-based algorithms, which have become

00:20.200 --> 00:25.120
quite popular in the field of robotics and robot autonomy.

00:25.120 --> 00:29.800
In particular, as you know, the problem of motion planning entails finding a sequence

00:29.800 --> 00:37.360
of actions that drive a robot from an initial position to a goal region, while avoiding

00:37.360 --> 00:43.960
obstacles, respecting the dynamics of the system, and possibly optimizing a cost function.

00:43.960 --> 00:50.240
Last time I had as an image of a motion planning problem an image of a discretized word, because

00:50.240 --> 00:52.880
we focus more on grid-based approaches.

00:52.880 --> 00:57.560
Here I have an image of motion planning where we have a continuous path, because today we're

00:57.560 --> 01:04.880
going to look at algorithms that reason about the continuous formulation of motion planning,

01:04.880 --> 01:08.160
where you're reasoning about the continuous path.

01:08.160 --> 01:11.680
In particular, the goal of today is to learn about the class of algorithms referred to

01:11.680 --> 01:14.600
as a sampling-based motion planning algorithms.

01:14.600 --> 01:23.160
A good reading for the lecture of today is chapter 5 in the book of Steve Lavallee called

01:23.160 --> 01:26.920
The Planning Algorithms.

01:26.920 --> 01:31.720
So let's start from where we ended last time.

01:31.720 --> 01:36.840
A critical point that I already made a few times, but I want to make one more time, is

01:36.840 --> 01:41.920
that while the motion planning problem is defined in the workspace, you want to find

01:41.920 --> 01:45.880
a trajectory that does not collide with obstacles.

01:45.880 --> 01:51.840
The procedure, the process of building a trajectory really happens in another space, the configuration

01:51.840 --> 01:58.160
space, the space of configurations that a robot can have.

01:58.160 --> 02:04.640
Like in the case of a unicycle, where you have as a configuration the XY position of

02:04.640 --> 02:10.360
the unicycle and the heading of the unicycle, the configuration space is three-dimensional.

02:10.360 --> 02:15.920
Now the difficulty is that translating constraints that are defined in the workspace, in the

02:15.920 --> 02:22.600
real world, into constraints in the configuration space is, in general, quite difficult.

02:22.600 --> 02:28.560
In particular, to better explain this difficulty, this is the same sequence of figures that

02:28.560 --> 02:29.560
I had last time.

02:29.560 --> 02:37.040
I'm going to show how the difficulty in making this translation possible increases as the

02:37.040 --> 02:40.120
number of dimensions of the configuration space increases.

02:40.120 --> 02:44.600
Let's consider first the case of a triangular robot that wants to maneuver from initial

02:44.600 --> 02:47.120
configuration to final configuration.

02:47.120 --> 02:52.280
Here we're allowing the robot to only move in the vertical direction and in the horizontal

02:52.280 --> 02:56.080
direction, meaning that the configuration space is two-dimensional and the workspace

02:56.080 --> 02:58.920
is two-dimensional as well.

02:58.920 --> 03:04.040
So the way that motion planning works is that we shrink the robot to point its configuration

03:04.040 --> 03:07.280
and accordingly we enlarge the obstacles.

03:07.280 --> 03:11.560
In this case it's fairly simple, so what we would do would be to slice the shape of

03:11.560 --> 03:18.360
the robot around the obstacles and then the representation of the obstacle in the configuration

03:18.360 --> 03:25.360
space of the robot would be this gray area plus the purple area, with the idea that if

03:25.360 --> 03:31.320
the configuration of the robot, in this case taken to be the XY position of this corner

03:31.320 --> 03:38.200
of the robot, is not in neither the purple area nor in the gray area, then the robot

03:38.200 --> 03:42.480
in the workspace, what we really care about, is not going to be in condition with the obstacle

03:42.480 --> 03:43.480
at all.

03:43.480 --> 03:51.560
Does it make sense?

03:51.560 --> 03:55.120
So we do the same for the other obstacle.

03:55.120 --> 04:00.000
We do the same with respect to the boundaries of the environment, so we have to crop the

04:00.000 --> 04:04.560
boundaries to make sure that our obstacle doesn't get outside of the boundaries of the

04:04.560 --> 04:05.560
environment.

04:05.560 --> 04:11.160
And now the problem has been translated into the problem of finding a path for a point

04:11.160 --> 04:16.320
which represents the configuration of the robot, which as I said, would represent the

04:16.320 --> 04:25.400
XY coordinates of this vertex of the triangular robot, then we just find the path knowing

04:25.400 --> 04:33.440
that if the XY position of this vertex is collision free in the configuration space,

04:33.440 --> 04:38.080
then the robot is going to be obstacle free in the real space, in the workspace.

04:38.080 --> 04:45.880
So these are all good and fine, but now let's assume that we start considering a more complicated

04:45.880 --> 04:50.920
model for the triangular robot, whereby apart from just translating, the robot can also

04:50.920 --> 04:51.920
rotate.

04:51.920 --> 04:56.240
So the configuration is both XY and theta, the heading angle.

04:56.240 --> 05:02.160
In this case, the workspace is still two dimensional, but now the configuration space is three dimensional,

05:02.160 --> 05:08.960
XY and theta, which means that we will need to carry out the same sliding procedure for

05:08.960 --> 05:18.200
each fixed value of theta and incrementally build a 3D representation of the obstacles

05:18.200 --> 05:19.820
in the configuration space.

05:19.820 --> 05:23.740
So even though the obstacles in the workspace are two dimensional, now in the configuration

05:23.740 --> 05:29.100
space, they will look something like this, fairly complicated three dimensional objects.

05:29.100 --> 05:35.180
You can imagine how this procedure, as the configuration vector becomes larger and larger,

05:35.180 --> 05:42.320
or if you start accounting for dynamics, becomes extremely complicated.

05:42.320 --> 05:47.300
So the key point here is that when the motion planning problem is described in the real

05:47.300 --> 05:52.780
world, also referred to as the workspace, it really lives in another space, the configuration

05:52.780 --> 05:59.100
space, whereby the robot has been shrunk to a point and the obstacles have been enlarged

05:59.100 --> 06:03.500
accordingly and represented in the configuration space.

06:03.500 --> 06:07.860
So having said that, there are two main approaches to continuous motion planning, continuous

06:07.860 --> 06:11.700
in the sense that we reason about the continuous trajectory.

06:11.740 --> 06:15.900
The first approach to continuous motion planning, which is what we saw toward the end of the

06:15.900 --> 06:23.820
lecture on Tuesday, whereby we build data structures, like for example, cell decompositions

06:23.820 --> 06:30.940
that discretely approximate the continuous formulation of the problem without neglecting

06:30.940 --> 06:34.980
any information that is necessary for the problem.

06:34.980 --> 06:39.280
This is, in principle, the ideal approach.

06:39.280 --> 06:44.200
The disadvantage of this approach is that in order to work, it requires the explicit

06:44.200 --> 06:51.240
representation of the obstacles in the configuration space, which, as I said, is extremely challenging.

06:51.240 --> 06:55.280
To build the cell decomposition, you need to have explicit representation of the obstacles

06:55.280 --> 06:59.160
in the configuration space, which is very challenging.

06:59.160 --> 07:04.840
This motivated the development of a second class of algorithms, simply based algorithms,

07:04.840 --> 07:10.400
which only implicitly represent the obstacles in the configuration space.

07:10.400 --> 07:15.840
And this is what makes them really work for complex motion planning problems.

07:15.840 --> 07:21.160
I will discuss in a second what implicit representation of an obstacle in the configuration space

07:21.160 --> 07:22.120
means.

07:27.080 --> 07:30.440
The idea is as follows.

07:30.480 --> 07:36.280
Using the obstacles from the workspace to the configuration space is hard.

07:36.280 --> 07:43.680
But what is easy is to say, if a robot is a given configuration, say x, y, theta, would

07:43.680 --> 07:49.260
the robot be in collision with an obstacle in the workspace, yes or no?

07:49.260 --> 07:55.180
Such yes or no type of question can be easily answered by using a collision checker.

07:55.180 --> 08:01.100
So the idea of a sampling-based algorithm is to avoid the explicit representation of

08:01.100 --> 08:06.380
obstacles in the configuration space, and instead, probe the configuration space with

08:06.380 --> 08:10.740
a set of samples that are drawn randomly.

08:10.740 --> 08:17.700
For each sample, which is a specific instantiation of the configuration vector, we call a collision

08:17.700 --> 08:23.500
checker asking the question, if the robot were to be at this configuration, would the

08:23.500 --> 08:26.200
robot be in a collision, yes or no?

08:26.200 --> 08:31.180
If the sample is collision-free, then we retain it.

08:31.180 --> 08:34.660
If it actually will lead to a collision, then we discard it.

08:34.660 --> 08:40.060
And then by using these samples, we build a roadmap or some variation of a roadmap that

08:40.060 --> 08:42.260
we can leverage to compute a path.

08:42.260 --> 08:48.680
I will give you explicit examples of algorithms instantiating this idea in a second.

08:48.860 --> 08:54.660
Overall, this is the key idea behind the sampling-based algorithms.

08:54.660 --> 09:00.160
As I said, to capture the structure of the configuration space by random sampling, then

09:00.160 --> 09:09.480
use a black box component, a collision checker, to check if every sample configuration will

09:09.480 --> 09:11.880
lead to a collision, yes or no?

09:11.880 --> 09:16.760
And then use such a set of samples in order to build a roadmap.

09:17.240 --> 09:23.720
A good reference for this is a paper, 2011, from Steve Laval, the same author of the book

09:23.720 --> 09:27.960
I mentioned before.

09:27.960 --> 09:35.720
Now before we go into specific instantiation of this idea by discussing some popular algorithms

09:35.720 --> 09:40.520
implemented in this idea, let me say that the main advantages of this class of algorithms,

09:40.520 --> 09:46.720
which has made them quite popular in the robotics domain, is that, as you will see, these algorithms

09:46.720 --> 09:48.440
are conceptually simple.

09:48.440 --> 09:53.240
Remember, when we were talking about combinatorial algorithms, we were talking about doing a

09:53.240 --> 09:59.480
set of decompositions that require complicated geometric structures that require quite a

09:59.480 --> 10:02.040
bit of coding and so on?

10:02.040 --> 10:05.800
These algorithms are super simple to implement, say, 50 lines of code.

10:05.800 --> 10:06.800
That's it.

10:06.800 --> 10:11.680
Very difficult to make mistakes with them, which is a big plus.

10:11.680 --> 10:14.320
Second of all, they are quite flexible.

10:14.400 --> 10:22.560
For example, with the combinatorial algorithms, the set decomposition approach is really dependent

10:22.560 --> 10:26.600
on how you represent your obstacles, like in a polygonal way.

10:26.600 --> 10:32.760
It really depends on the specifics of your robots, whether it has dynamics or not dynamics.

10:32.760 --> 10:38.080
These algorithms, as we will see, can be applied very broadly.

10:38.080 --> 10:40.800
And in particular, they can be applied beyond the geometric case.

10:40.840 --> 10:45.840
They can be applied to the case where we account for dynamics in a motion planning procedure,

10:45.840 --> 10:50.040
can be extended to the case where we also account for uncertainty in environment, even

10:50.040 --> 10:52.920
though we won't see it in this class, and so on.

10:52.920 --> 10:57.120
There are disadvantages, as for everything in engineering.

10:57.120 --> 11:03.040
First of all, as you will see, a key question is, how many samples do we need in order to

11:03.040 --> 11:06.040
accurately cover the configuration space?

11:06.040 --> 11:11.320
In general, there is no rigorous answer to this question, which means that you still

11:11.320 --> 11:19.600
need some experience in order to select a good enough number of samples that you want

11:19.600 --> 11:20.600
to use.

11:20.600 --> 11:26.120
Second of all, if there is a path, these algorithms will most likely find it.

11:26.120 --> 11:30.760
And actually, we're going to have more precise statements about this.

11:30.760 --> 11:35.700
But if a path does not exist, in general, these algorithms won't tell you that it does

11:35.700 --> 11:36.700
not exist.

11:36.700 --> 11:40.900
The algorithm will continue running, and then you will continue waiting, but it will never

11:40.900 --> 11:44.280
terminate saying, sorry, no path exists.

11:44.280 --> 11:45.280
Which is annoying, right?

11:45.280 --> 11:49.940
From an algorithm, you would like to know that if a solution exists, it finds it, and

11:49.940 --> 11:52.380
if it does not exist, it tells you that it does not exist.

11:52.380 --> 11:57.000
These algorithms, unfortunately, do not tell you that a solution does not exist.

11:57.000 --> 12:03.140
But in reality, this is a mild disadvantage, because in practice, if an algorithm runs

12:03.140 --> 12:07.300
for a long time, you know that most likely a solution does not exist, and that's what

12:07.300 --> 12:09.960
you basically claim.

12:09.960 --> 12:15.780
So today, we're going to discuss three main topics in the general umbrella of sampling-based

12:15.780 --> 12:16.780
motion planning algorithms.

12:16.780 --> 12:24.020
First, I'm going to discuss representative algorithms that are sampling-based and address

12:24.020 --> 12:29.060
the problem of geometric planning, meaning we try to find a path that avoids obstacles,

12:29.060 --> 12:34.220
but we do not account for dynamics in the motion planning procedure.

12:34.220 --> 12:38.740
Then we consider the case where we actually account for dynamics in the motion planning

12:38.740 --> 12:39.740
procedure.

12:39.740 --> 12:44.620
In this case, motion planning is referred to as a kino-dynamic planning to make it explicit

12:44.620 --> 12:46.660
that we're counting for dynamics.

12:46.660 --> 12:54.780
And finally, I will discuss a fairly natural question, which is, does the probing of the

12:54.780 --> 12:59.340
configuration space through the sampling procedure need to be random?

12:59.340 --> 13:08.020
As I said, since the first description of these algorithms used randomized sampling,

13:08.020 --> 13:16.180
but a natural question is, does the probing sampling sequence need to be random or can

13:16.180 --> 13:19.940
be done actually deterministically?

13:19.940 --> 13:22.220
Let's start with the geometric case.

13:22.220 --> 13:28.900
Any questions so far?

13:28.900 --> 13:33.460
So the key concept that is a bit subtle, and I will say that a number of roboticists out

13:33.460 --> 13:38.060
there actually confuse this concept a little bit, is the main difference between combinatorial

13:38.060 --> 13:42.700
and sampling-based is that the combinatorial algorithms explicitly represent obstacles

13:42.700 --> 13:44.620
in the configuration space.

13:44.620 --> 13:50.260
Sampling-based relies on implicit representation of obstacles in the configuration space through

13:50.260 --> 13:59.220
this notion of sampling, whereby you just throw some possible instantiation of configurations

13:59.220 --> 14:06.140
and for each one, you call a collision checker to see if those samples are in collision or

14:06.140 --> 14:07.140
not.

14:07.140 --> 14:11.940
By doing so, you only implicitly represent the obstacles because the obstacles are only

14:11.940 --> 14:23.580
represented by the samples that have been declared respectively collision-free or not.

14:23.580 --> 14:28.300
So there are two main approaches in the field of sampling-based planning.

14:28.300 --> 14:33.420
The first approach is referred to as a multi-query planning.

14:33.420 --> 14:39.860
Multi-query means that this class of algorithms give you a data structure that can be used

14:39.860 --> 14:45.900
to solve any motion planning instance that you want.

14:45.900 --> 14:51.940
Any means for any pair of origin and destination, you can use this data structure to find a

14:51.940 --> 14:52.940
path.

14:52.940 --> 14:57.300
Single-query instead of planners that allow you to solve only one instance of the motion

14:57.300 --> 15:04.420
planning problem, basically for one specific origin and goal region pair.

15:04.420 --> 15:08.060
Depending on your application, one might be preferable to the other one.

15:08.060 --> 15:15.580
So the first algorithm that was proposed in the multi-query setting was the PRM algorithm,

15:15.580 --> 15:20.780
Probabilistic Roadmap, which was devised about 20 years ago by Lydia Kavaraki and her

15:20.780 --> 15:27.740
co-authors and has seen a lot of development in the subsequent 20 years.

15:27.740 --> 15:32.000
The other approach, the single-query, is dominated by a class of algorithms referred

15:32.000 --> 15:38.840
to as RRTs, Rapidly Exploring Random Trees, which was proposed a few years later with

15:38.840 --> 15:39.840
respect to PRM.

15:39.840 --> 15:45.760
It was proposed in 2001 by Lavallee and here there is a typo, it should be Kufner.

15:45.760 --> 15:50.840
Lavallee is the same author of the book that I mentioned before.

15:50.840 --> 15:56.280
And this algorithm as well has seen a lot of development in the past 15 years or so.

15:56.280 --> 16:02.340
So what we're going to do now is to discuss in terms PRM and RRT, discuss the relative

16:02.340 --> 16:08.460
advantages and disadvantages, and then discuss also some alternatives.

16:08.460 --> 16:10.820
So let's consider PRM first.

16:10.820 --> 16:14.600
The idea of PRM is fairly simple.

16:14.600 --> 16:21.500
So let's say that this is the configuration space of our robot.

16:21.500 --> 16:25.080
White means that this region is collision free.

16:25.080 --> 16:30.720
So if the configuration of the robot is this point, remember that the configuration represents

16:30.720 --> 16:40.500
the value for a configuration of the robot, specifically in the case of a unicycle, specific

16:40.500 --> 16:43.300
values for x, y, and theta.

16:43.300 --> 16:50.860
So if this configuration, so white means free space, gray means a set of configurations

16:50.860 --> 16:54.960
that will lead to a collision for the robot in the workspace.

16:54.960 --> 17:04.260
The way that PRM, in sampling base, we assume that we do not have an explicit characterization

17:04.260 --> 17:06.960
of the free region of the configuration space.

17:06.960 --> 17:13.620
So I'm showing it here because, of course, that notion exists, but the algorithm does

17:13.620 --> 17:15.320
not have access to it.

17:15.320 --> 17:21.820
So what the algorithm does is to throw a bunch of possible configurations.

17:21.820 --> 17:26.380
Each one, again, corresponds to a given pose of the robot in the workspace.

17:26.380 --> 17:29.780
For each one, we call a collision checker.

17:29.780 --> 17:35.740
So we say, if the robot were to have this pose, would the robot be in a collision?

17:35.740 --> 17:36.740
Yes or no?

17:36.740 --> 17:40.260
In this case, the answer is yes, here you will be in a collision.

17:40.260 --> 17:43.500
No, here you would not be in a collision.

17:43.500 --> 17:50.440
So you discard all these samples that will lead to a collision, and you keep those ones

17:50.440 --> 17:54.520
that would ensure that the robot is collision free.

17:54.520 --> 17:59.160
And that's our implicit representation of the free configuration space.

17:59.160 --> 18:04.480
The robot does not have access to these boundaries, but has an implicit characterization of what

18:04.480 --> 18:11.520
is free through the samples that have been declared collision free.

18:11.520 --> 18:20.720
Then what we do is for each sample, we try to connect that sample to neighbors.

18:20.720 --> 18:25.400
Basically we try to answer the question, can I go from this sample to this sample in a

18:25.400 --> 18:27.040
way that is collision free?

18:27.040 --> 18:31.720
So in the case where we're solving a geometric problem, the best way to connect two points

18:31.720 --> 18:34.640
is through a straight line.

18:34.640 --> 18:38.580
So for each point, we look at the neighbor, for each sample, we look at the neighborhood

18:38.580 --> 18:46.260
around that sample, and we try to connect that sample to all its neighbors.

18:46.260 --> 18:54.460
In particular, we look for each sample x at all samples x prime that are within a ball

18:54.460 --> 18:57.540
of radius r.

18:57.540 --> 19:03.380
So you might say, why don't you try to connect each sample to any other sample?

19:03.380 --> 19:09.620
In principle, that will give you a very dense graph that you could use to plan high quality

19:09.620 --> 19:10.880
paths.

19:10.880 --> 19:12.480
So that seems reasonable.

19:12.480 --> 19:18.000
So why in practice people do not consider as radius in order to establish connections

19:18.000 --> 19:25.900
to neighbors the full diameter of the environment?

19:25.900 --> 19:31.440
Yeah, because that would require you to evaluate a lot of connections.

19:31.440 --> 19:35.600
For each one of these connections, you will need to call a collision checker to make

19:35.600 --> 19:38.400
sure that this connection is collision free.

19:38.400 --> 19:42.640
Remember that the robot does not have an explicit characterization of the obstacles in the configuration

19:42.640 --> 19:43.640
space.

19:43.640 --> 19:47.960
So in order to check whether this edge is collision free, what in practice you had to

19:47.960 --> 19:57.000
do is to discretize this edge in multiple configurations for each one called a collision

19:57.000 --> 19:58.420
checker.

19:58.420 --> 20:04.940
So you can see that the more connections you're trying to establish, the more computation

20:04.940 --> 20:09.540
that you have to do up to a point where this cannot be run and you have time anymore, which

20:09.540 --> 20:14.220
means that the algorithm is not useful anymore in general.

20:14.220 --> 20:22.060
So you try to limit the size of these radius in such a way that you still connect to a

20:22.060 --> 20:26.600
representative number of neighbors, but you don't do too much computation.

20:26.600 --> 20:33.160
We discuss later on how to choose that radius in a way that represents a good compromise

20:33.160 --> 20:37.240
between solution quality and computation.

20:37.240 --> 20:39.780
So you establish these connections.

20:39.780 --> 20:46.520
And once you've done that, what you have is a roadmap in your free configuration space.

20:46.520 --> 20:57.040
And for any motion planning instance, which means for each pair of goal and starting

20:57.040 --> 21:02.600
configuration and final configuration, what you do is attach your initial configuration

21:02.600 --> 21:09.560
to the closest sample in the roadmap, your goal configuration to the closest sample in

21:09.560 --> 21:12.080
the roadmap, like this.

21:12.080 --> 21:16.760
And then you plan a path by using one of the pathfinding algorithms that we discussed

21:16.760 --> 21:21.400
last time, like the Jextra or A star and so on.

21:21.400 --> 21:23.220
And that's it.

21:23.220 --> 21:27.460
As you can see, this is a very simple algorithm.

21:27.460 --> 21:34.080
So once you have a collision checking module, which oftentimes is relatively easy, the collision

21:34.080 --> 21:39.000
checking module only has to answer the question, if the robot is a dispose, is the robot going

21:39.000 --> 21:40.000
to be in collision?

21:40.040 --> 21:44.440
The robot is just evaluating the intersection in general between polygons.

21:44.440 --> 21:48.560
It's still computationally intense, but it's not the end of the world.

21:48.560 --> 21:55.560
So once you have that in place, once you have collision checking in place, then coding a

21:55.560 --> 21:59.840
PRM algorithm is really a matter of 40, 50 lines of code.

21:59.840 --> 22:00.840
Super easy.

22:00.840 --> 22:01.840
Yeah.

22:01.960 --> 22:04.480
Why do you only connect the certain end to the closest nodes?

22:04.480 --> 22:09.600
Like, what if it leads you to go backwards first, or what if the graph is disconnected

22:09.600 --> 22:12.720
and connected to one side instead of the other side?

22:12.720 --> 22:13.220
Sure.

22:13.220 --> 22:13.880
That can happen.

22:13.880 --> 22:18.440
The only problem is that you don't have any.

22:18.440 --> 22:23.440
So sometimes you have additional information that can guide you in that choice.

22:23.440 --> 22:27.480
But usually, in absence of other information, this is the best you can do.

22:27.480 --> 22:36.240
When you pick your initial and final configuration, and you go to the closest node, basically,

22:36.240 --> 22:37.720
do you need to check that streamline?

22:37.720 --> 22:38.720
Oh, yes, absolutely.

22:38.720 --> 22:43.920
So it might be very well the case that this is actually, there is an obstacle here.

22:43.920 --> 22:48.240
So there is an obstacle in the workspace that translates into the configuration space will

22:48.240 --> 22:53.040
lead you to a gray area here, which would invalidate this edge, which means that now

22:53.040 --> 22:55.040
you have to consider the second best.

22:55.040 --> 22:56.040
Yeah.

22:57.040 --> 23:03.240
Would it be more efficient to do more random samplings closer to the robot, and then less

23:03.240 --> 23:05.240
further away from the robot?

23:05.240 --> 23:09.840
Yeah, so there are a number of parameters that we haven't discussed about this algorithm.

23:09.840 --> 23:17.440
In particular, how we select the radius, how densely we sample, something that will remedy

23:17.440 --> 23:21.400
to the issue that you mentioned, and with which distribution.

23:21.400 --> 23:26.200
So here, I just show a uniform distribution.

23:26.200 --> 23:32.840
But in reality, as you said, a much better way to sample, and that's what people actually

23:32.840 --> 23:39.080
do in practice, is to do non-uniform sampling informed by some players.

23:39.080 --> 23:44.160
At the very least, you try to sample more closely toward your robot, toward your goal

23:44.160 --> 23:45.160
region.

23:45.160 --> 23:51.960
Sometimes, you have some understanding about what are the best pathways in the configuration

23:51.960 --> 23:53.120
space, and so on.

23:53.120 --> 23:57.680
A common trend, and also something that we work with a bit in the lab, is to use data-driven

23:57.680 --> 24:02.240
methods, so basically machine learning methods, to learn good sampling distribution.

24:02.240 --> 24:07.480
So basically, you solve one million motion planning problems, and then you have a neural

24:07.480 --> 24:14.160
net that learns, given as an input, the representation of the obstacles in the workspace, what is

24:14.160 --> 24:17.920
a good sampling distribution in the configuration space.

24:17.920 --> 24:18.920
So that's an excellent question.

24:18.920 --> 24:23.200
And just to make sure I understand, so we still presume that we know where all the obstacles

24:23.200 --> 24:24.200
are in the workspace?

24:24.200 --> 24:25.200
Yes.

24:25.200 --> 24:26.200
Yeah.

24:26.200 --> 24:27.200
That's the assumption of-

24:27.200 --> 24:28.440
That's the input to the algorithm.

24:28.440 --> 24:34.640
What we do not assume we know is the representation of them in the configuration space.

24:34.640 --> 24:40.100
Then the other version is, what if the obstacles are time-varying in the workspace?

24:40.140 --> 24:46.180
What if we have uncertainty about where the obstacles are in the workspace, and so on?

24:46.180 --> 24:50.020
And there are answers to all those questions, but those are beyond the scope of this class.

24:50.020 --> 24:59.300
Would it ever be worthwhile to somewhere down during the path to recalculate and resample?

24:59.300 --> 25:00.300
Yes, absolutely.

25:00.300 --> 25:06.460
So the way these algorithms are run, and that's why they need to run fast, is that you compute

25:06.460 --> 25:14.100
this path, you execute the first part of it, and then you recompute the path in a receding

25:14.100 --> 25:19.380
horizon matter, or in a repeated optimization matter.

25:19.380 --> 25:26.300
So it really depends on the tempo of your dynamics, but usually, as a rule of thumb,

25:26.300 --> 25:31.780
you want these algorithms to run between 200 milliseconds to a couple of seconds in order

25:31.780 --> 25:37.060
to re-optimize quickly enough to account for variations in your environment.

25:39.620 --> 25:43.940
How does the fluency checker actually work, because don't we still need like a full representation

