showed in the diagram before, because we would still need to find the intersection between-

25:53.540 --> 25:55.940
But in the workspace, not in the computation space.

25:55.940 --> 25:57.060
In the workspace, yeah.

25:57.060 --> 26:02.380
Yes, well, so the problem becomes a purely geometric problem.

26:02.380 --> 26:07.500
For example, sometimes there are some libraries to do that, or you can also use bullet and so on.

26:07.500 --> 26:10.220
But the idea is that it's a purely geometric problem in the workspace.

26:10.220 --> 26:13.060
You don't have to do any complicated transformation.

26:13.060 --> 26:18.500
What you have is that each configuration corresponds to a pose of the robot, xy position, position

26:18.500 --> 26:21.260
of the joint angles if you're a manipulator, and so on.

26:21.260 --> 26:26.660
So now you have a certain configuration of the robot in the workspace, you have an obstacle,

26:26.660 --> 26:32.620
and then you call bullet or something else to say, are these two geometric entities intersecting or not?

26:32.620 --> 26:35.460
How you do that is not entirely straightforward.

26:35.460 --> 26:39.020
But by now there are libraries that do that fairly reliably.

26:39.020 --> 26:43.140
Usually, as a roboticist, you do not implement collision checker.

26:43.140 --> 26:45.660
There are libraries out there that do a very good job.

26:45.660 --> 26:49.060
What you do is to implement these algorithms.

26:49.060 --> 26:53.820
Out of curiosity, do those algorithms convert the robot into the workspace

26:53.820 --> 26:58.980
and then check the configuration collisions in the workspace, where they translate the obstacles into the configuration?

26:58.980 --> 27:00.540
No, no, no.

27:00.540 --> 27:04.340
But that's the whole point, that you don't want to do this translation because it's in general intractable.

27:04.340 --> 27:06.900
So it's easier to translate the robot into the workspace than that.

27:06.900 --> 27:13.740
We know that you translate, because every point indeed corresponds to a pose in the configuration space.

27:13.740 --> 27:17.220
So then you just, well, yes, OK.

27:17.220 --> 27:23.180
So if you will, you translate that pose into a set of points in the workspace that are taken up by the robot.

27:23.220 --> 27:24.660
Yeah, that's how they work.

27:24.660 --> 27:30.020
So the collision checking is done in the workspace, not in the configuration space.

27:30.020 --> 27:30.700
Cool.

27:30.700 --> 27:40.580
I like these questions because it really means that you understood this business of configuration space and workspace,

27:40.580 --> 27:44.460
and also that I'm a good teacher, that I was able to communicate that to you.

27:44.460 --> 27:47.660
So this graph sits in the configuration space?

27:47.660 --> 27:49.740
Yes, absolutely.

27:49.740 --> 27:54.460
OK, that question actually makes me more worried that I'm a good teacher because, yes, absolutely.

27:54.460 --> 27:57.220
Although the point of this is that this is a configuration space.

27:57.220 --> 28:06.660
This is the free space, the free area in the configuration space that we only implicitly present through this sampling scheme.

28:06.660 --> 28:16.580
So you said that with these methods, we are, like this method runs infinitely if we can't find the path to find it?

28:16.620 --> 28:21.380
Yeah, because say that we don't find a solution.

28:21.380 --> 28:29.100
That you don't know whether you don't find a solution because you don't have a problem scheme that is fine enough,

28:29.100 --> 28:31.740
or because actually there is no feasible solution.

28:31.740 --> 28:40.180
