29:41.100 --> 29:43.260
So we try to establish this connection doesn't work.

29:43.260 --> 29:44.980
We try to establish this connection doesn't work.

29:44.980 --> 29:48.100
This does not work, this does not work, this does not work.

29:48.100 --> 29:51.340
The algorithm will say, I can't find a path.

29:51.340 --> 30:02.700
But if you sample more densely, then I can find a path to get out.

30:02.700 --> 30:07.660
So by the way, these situations are referred to as bug traps in the motion planning literature.

30:07.660 --> 30:10.540
Notoriously difficult to deal with.

30:10.540 --> 30:18.780
They happen sometimes with planning for robotic manipulators where you have to maneuver in very tight spaces,

30:18.780 --> 30:23.820
for example, to place a cup in the refrigerator and so on.

30:23.820 --> 30:27.260
All right, so that's PRM in a nutshell.

30:27.260 --> 30:36.220
So easy algorithm to implement, very general, tends to work pretty well, reliable, what they use.

30:36.220 --> 30:43.900
It's a multi-query algorithm because if now you give me a different initial and goal position,

30:43.900 --> 30:46.900
I would just connect them to the roadmap and I would solve the problem.

30:46.900 --> 30:52.500
So it's not really specific to the initial and goal position.

30:52.500 --> 30:59.460
The other class of, well, the other algorithm that is very popular under the umbrella of sampling-based algorithm

30:59.460 --> 31:07.900
is the so-called RRT algorithms, which is a single-query algorithm that works by growing a tree or path

31:07.900 --> 31:12.740
rooted at the starting configuration.

31:12.740 --> 31:15.780
In particular, the way it works is as follows.

31:15.780 --> 31:25.300
Let's assume that we have already grown a portfolio of a path.

31:25.300 --> 31:28.780
It's really a tree which is rooted at the initial configuration.

31:28.780 --> 31:36.060
The way it works is that we build this tree iteratively by sampling one configuration at a time.

31:36.060 --> 31:41.900
So, for example, here we sample a new configuration that had one.

31:41.900 --> 31:47.180
We collision check whether that configuration is collision-free or not.

31:47.180 --> 31:50.940
In this case, it's collision-free, so we keep it.

31:50.940 --> 31:58.820
Then we find the nearest point in the tree to that configuration.

31:58.820 --> 32:08.340
Then we try to extend the tree either all the way to the newly sampled configuration or a fraction of the way.

32:08.340 --> 32:14.980
Usually you don't want to extend it all the way because otherwise it will lead to trees with very long branches

32:14.980 --> 32:18.260
that might lead to paths that are highly non-optimal.

32:18.260 --> 32:24.380
So usually you just extend by, say, 10% of the distance or so.

32:24.380 --> 32:27.380
Then you collision check this edge.

32:27.380 --> 32:30.780
In this case, this edge is collision-free, and then you keep it.

32:30.780 --> 32:36.220
And then you have added a new tiny branch to your tree.

32:36.220 --> 32:37.500
And then you keep going.

32:37.500 --> 32:44.460
So initially you start just from the root of the tree, which is the initial configuration.

32:44.460 --> 32:49.500
And then you incrementally proceed with this process.

32:49.500 --> 32:52.620
The sample new configuration.

32:52.620 --> 32:59.500
Here, in this case, this configuration will be in collision.

32:59.500 --> 33:06.580
But you still try to extend to it, at least in a way that your edge is collision-free.

33:06.580 --> 33:09.220
And then you keep this edge, and so on.

33:09.220 --> 33:16.180
You keep doing that, and eventually you will get to the goal.

33:16.180 --> 33:18.060
Does that make sense?

33:18.060 --> 33:20.820
So this is an incremental algorithm,

33:20.820 --> 33:25.340
whereas the PRM algorithm is a batch algorithm.

33:25.340 --> 33:29.540
In the PRM, you throw n samples, and then you build a roadmap.

33:29.540 --> 33:35.460
Here you throw one sample at a time, and you incrementally build a tree.

33:35.460 --> 33:41.140
It's single query, because we had built the tree at the initial configuration.

33:41.140 --> 33:44.940
So if now you give me another initial configuration,

33:44.940 --> 33:49.140
it's unclear how I will reuse this data structure to plan a path.

33:49.140 --> 33:53.500
There are ways, actually, to make RT also multi-query.

33:53.500 --> 33:56.260
But in general, it's a single-query algorithm.

33:56.260 --> 33:59.940
So this is the procedure in more mathematical terms.

33:59.940 --> 34:02.660
We sample a new configuration, which we call xrun.

34:02.660 --> 34:04.300
Run stands for randomize.

34:04.340 --> 34:07.380
We find the nearest vertex in the tree to xrun.

34:07.380 --> 34:12.980
We extend the tree from the nearest neighbor to xrun,

34:12.980 --> 34:16.940
in such a way that the extended edge is collision-free.

34:16.940 --> 34:21.300
And then we update the tree with a new branch, and then we repeat.

34:21.300 --> 34:27.060
In general, if you do this process, it would be very unlikely,

34:27.060 --> 34:30.220
and actually it will have a probability 0,

34:30.220 --> 34:32.860
that at some point you sample the goal tree,

34:32.860 --> 34:34.540
and you get to the goal tree.

34:34.540 --> 34:39.780
So RT is usually implemented with the so-called goal-biasing strategy,

34:39.780 --> 34:46.300
whereby every few times you sample as a configuration

34:46.300 --> 34:47.820
the goal configuration.

34:47.820 --> 34:50.020
So basically, in that way, you try to see

34:50.020 --> 34:52.780
if you can make a direct connection to the goal.

34:52.780 --> 35:00.180
So say, every 20 samples, you sample non-randomly the goal configuration,

35:00.180 --> 35:04.020
and then you try to extend the tree toward that goal.

35:04.020 --> 35:06.700
It is referred to goal-biasing, because doing so,

35:06.700 --> 35:09.860
you're biasing the growth of the tree toward the goal.

35:09.860 --> 35:12.220
Practically, if you don't implement goal-biasing,

35:12.220 --> 35:13.780
this algorithm really doesn't work.

35:13.780 --> 35:17.060
If you implement goal-biasing, this algorithm works pretty well.

35:17.060 --> 35:20.820
Do you want to check the goal if you're close enough?

35:20.820 --> 35:22.380
Like, if you're really far away from the goal,

35:22.380 --> 35:23.460
would you actually want to check?

35:23.460 --> 35:25.340
Because then that edge would be really long.

35:25.340 --> 35:30.140
Yeah, so there are about 3,000 papers

35:30.140 --> 35:34.660
that are offspring of the original paper that proposed RT,

35:34.660 --> 35:38.420
with all sorts of variations on the theme.

35:38.420 --> 35:41.660
How much do we extend, when do we do it,

35:41.660 --> 35:45.180
how do we select this fraction, zillions of it.

35:45.180 --> 35:47.980
Because, again, this is an algorithm that you can implement

35:47.980 --> 35:51.900
in very few lines of code, very general, works very well in practice.

35:51.900 --> 35:55.460
Yes, absolutely, you could do that, as you could do many other things.

35:55.460 --> 35:58.380
We're going to talk about one particular variation,

35:58.380 --> 36:02.100
