36:49.980 --> 36:53.100
So one of the very first variations for PRM

36:53.140 --> 37:01.500
is to build the, so basically, sample.

37:01.500 --> 37:04.820
Then, so the variation that I'm referring to

37:04.820 --> 37:06.980
is referred to as a lazy PRM.

37:06.980 --> 37:10.140
So roughly speaking, the way that lazy PRM works

37:10.140 --> 37:12.660
is that once you have a sample, you just

37:12.660 --> 37:17.900
build the graph by using connection to your neighbors,

37:17.900 --> 37:21.260
but without collision checking those connections.

37:21.260 --> 37:26.140
Then you run A star, and you find the path assuming

37:26.140 --> 37:27.660
that there are no collisions.

37:27.660 --> 37:31.500
And then you collision check the edges along the path.

37:31.500 --> 37:34.380
If you have not found any edge in collision,

37:34.380 --> 37:35.700
that's the optimal path.

37:35.700 --> 37:39.980
If you do, you actually discard that edge,

37:39.980 --> 37:42.340
and you rerun A star.

37:42.340 --> 37:43.980
It's called lazy because in that case,

37:43.980 --> 37:46.340
you evaluate collision checking only

37:46.340 --> 37:49.340
when it is really needed to validate the terminal path.

37:49.340 --> 37:51.660
In that way, you would, as you said,

37:51.660 --> 37:55.300
avoid this wasted effort here.

37:55.300 --> 37:57.940
This effort will not be wasted if actually you

37:57.940 --> 38:02.700
plan to use the algorithm in a true multi-query setting,

38:02.700 --> 38:06.220
meaning that you want to use it for all possible source

38:06.220 --> 38:07.740
and goal destinations.

38:07.740 --> 38:10.740
But if you do not want to do it, then lazy PRM

38:10.740 --> 38:11.980
is the way to go.

38:11.980 --> 38:15.940
Indeed, lazy PRM is one of the most popular implementation

38:15.940 --> 38:16.580
of PRM.

