42:47.900 --> 42:54.460
So in order to put a sample in each quadrants in three

42:54.460 --> 42:57.700
dimensions.

42:57.700 --> 42:58.940
But that thing, right?

42:58.940 --> 43:02.180
So how many samples do you need?

43:02.180 --> 43:03.580
Right, so two to the three.

43:03.580 --> 43:05.700
And then you go to four dimensions.

43:05.700 --> 43:08.180
How do you call quadrant in four dimensions?

43:08.180 --> 43:08.740
I don't know.

43:08.740 --> 43:10.100
Anyway, 16.

43:10.100 --> 43:13.660
So basically, just roughly speaking,

43:13.660 --> 43:15.380
you need a number of samples that

43:15.380 --> 43:17.580
grows exponentially in the dimensions.

43:17.580 --> 43:19.760
So beyond 12, 13 does not work.

43:19.760 --> 43:23.620
So what you do in those cases is to either decompose the problem

43:23.620 --> 43:26.140
into subspaces.

43:26.140 --> 43:32.620
Or in many cases, the problem is not

43:32.660 --> 43:34.100
interesting in some of the dimensions,

43:34.100 --> 43:35.220
meaning that you don't have collisions

43:35.220 --> 43:36.380
in some of the dimensions.

43:36.380 --> 43:39.260
So you just collapse some of the dimensions.

43:39.260 --> 43:41.980
But this is beyond the scope of this class.

43:41.980 --> 43:45.780
For the system we deal with, these algorithms are fine.

43:45.780 --> 43:49.220
In particular, in this class, we deal mostly

43:49.220 --> 43:51.980
between three and eight dimensions.

43:51.980 --> 43:54.980
So we're good.

43:54.980 --> 43:57.820
So in practice, when you're finding the nearest vertex,

43:57.820 --> 44:00.300
do you use some fast running convergence?

44:00.340 --> 44:02.180
Yes, we're going to go back to what are

44:02.180 --> 44:03.740
the bottlenecks of these algorithms.

44:03.740 --> 44:07.100
Nearness enabled search is certainly one of the bottlenecks.

44:07.100 --> 44:10.540
And keep in mind that there are about,

44:10.540 --> 44:14.100
say, at ICRA, which is one of the most important robotics

44:14.100 --> 44:18.380
conferences that has about, say, 1,500 papers per year,

44:18.380 --> 44:20.900
there are about 200 papers on motion planning.

44:20.900 --> 44:22.860
So this is still a very active field.

44:22.860 --> 44:25.420
Particularly, for example, the question of scalability

44:25.420 --> 44:28.140
is still quite open.

44:28.140 --> 44:30.180
So what about theoretical guarantees?

44:30.180 --> 44:34.540
Unfortunately, in general, there are no theoretical guarantees

44:34.540 --> 44:35.500
in finite time.

44:35.500 --> 44:36.980
The only theoretical guarantees are

44:36.980 --> 44:40.100
with respect to the asymptotic behavior of these algorithms.

44:40.100 --> 44:43.580
Asymptotic means that we say something

44:43.580 --> 44:48.420
in the case where the number of samples goes to infinity.

44:48.420 --> 44:50.940
And in particular, the very first guarantees

44:50.940 --> 44:57.340
that were derived are that a PRM with a constant radius

44:57.340 --> 45:00.660
is guaranteed to find a feasible solution if one exists

45:00.660 --> 45:03.140
as the number of samples goes to infinity.

45:03.140 --> 45:07.340
So the probability that PRM does not find a solution if one

45:07.340 --> 45:12.860
exists goes to 0 as n goes to infinity.

45:12.860 --> 45:17.460
A similar theoretical guarantee exists for RRT.

45:17.460 --> 45:20.500
As the number of samples goes to infinity,

45:20.500 --> 45:26.860
then RRT is guaranteed to find a feasible trajectory, which

45:26.860 --> 45:27.340
is OK.

45:27.340 --> 45:29.420
I mean, it's not the best guarantee you can have.

45:29.420 --> 45:30.580
It's a probabilistic one.

45:30.580 --> 45:31.940
It's asymptotic.

45:31.940 --> 45:34.420
But it's better than nothing.

45:34.420 --> 45:37.260
In practice, as I said, they work pretty well.

45:37.260 --> 45:39.460
Now, all these guarantees are the guarantees

45:39.460 --> 45:42.740
that I just mentioned are with respect to feasibility.

45:42.740 --> 45:46.580
What about quality of the path?

45:46.580 --> 45:50.420
It turns out that RRT, in terms of quality,

45:50.420 --> 45:53.060
does not do a good job.

45:53.060 --> 45:56.940
In particular, oftentimes, as you will see in the P-sets,

45:56.940 --> 45:59.820
RRT produces trajectories that have

45:59.820 --> 46:04.140
a lot of zigzag, which means that the cost is large.

46:04.140 --> 46:06.340
Actually, you can prove mathematically

46:06.340 --> 46:10.180
that with non-negligible probability, in some instances,

46:10.180 --> 46:15.860
RRT provides a path whose cost is a factor away

46:15.860 --> 46:17.540
from the optimum cost.

46:17.540 --> 46:19.260
So for example, in this case, you

46:19.260 --> 46:22.300
can prove that it may happen that RRT

46:22.300 --> 46:24.420
with a non-zero probability will select

46:24.420 --> 46:28.900
as a path this very long one, as opposed to this blue one.

46:28.900 --> 46:30.620
The exact reason why that happens,

46:30.620 --> 46:31.980
we're not going to discuss it.

46:31.980 --> 46:34.420
But there are a couple of references out there

46:34.420 --> 46:37.820
that you can look at if you're interested.

46:37.820 --> 46:41.140
This has prompted, in the past 10 years or so,

46:41.140 --> 46:43.860
quite a bit of research in trying to understand

46:43.860 --> 46:52.900
what additional conditions should be done to the algorithms

46:52.900 --> 46:56.180
in order to ensure quality of the solution,

46:56.180 --> 46:58.500
meaning having a property that says

46:58.500 --> 47:01.020
that as the number of samples goes to infinity,

47:01.020 --> 47:03.300
then we recover not just a feasible solution,

47:03.300 --> 47:05.700
but actually an optimal solution.

47:05.700 --> 47:12.100
So it turns out that if you choose for PRM the radius

47:12.140 --> 47:16.100
with a scaling of log n over n to the power of 1 over d,

47:16.100 --> 47:20.300
then PRM is guaranteed to find, with probability 1,

47:20.300 --> 47:23.300
an optimal solution if 1 exists.

47:23.300 --> 47:26.580
So log n, so n here is the number of samples.

47:26.580 --> 47:29.540
D is the number of dimensions.

47:32.940 --> 47:35.340
As n goes to infinity, log n over n

47:35.340 --> 47:39.300
goes to 0, which makes sense, because basically,

47:39.340 --> 47:43.460
as n goes to infinity, so we have increasing more samples,

47:43.460 --> 47:45.420
we want to make the radius with respect

47:45.420 --> 47:48.300
to which we find neighbors smaller and smaller in order

47:48.300 --> 47:53.620
to contain the number of connections

47:53.620 --> 47:57.740
that we want to make that are computationally expensive.

47:57.740 --> 48:07.060
On the other hand, the ball has a radius in the dimension of n

48:07.100 --> 48:09.900
over d, which means that with this scaling,

48:09.900 --> 48:12.980
we are guaranteed that for all n,

48:12.980 --> 48:15.780
we have roughly log n neighbors.

48:15.780 --> 48:17.700
That's one last idea.

48:17.700 --> 48:20.420
So there's a good trade-off between computation

48:20.420 --> 48:23.300
and retaining enough connectivity of your roadmap.

48:23.300 --> 48:25.420
And in particular, it turns out that with this scaling

48:25.420 --> 48:27.340
or with the larger scaling, we are guaranteed

48:27.340 --> 48:30.020
to find an optimal solution.

48:30.020 --> 48:31.740
Now, it turns out, and this has been

48:31.740 --> 48:33.900
a very influential paper, so it turns out

48:33.940 --> 48:38.660
that it was a logical gap in the proof of this result.

48:38.660 --> 48:41.340
So this result, as stated, is not quite correct.

48:41.340 --> 48:45.380
So together with Kirill Solovey, who

48:45.380 --> 48:47.820
is a postdoc in my lab and a couple of students,

48:47.820 --> 48:50.500
I recently published a paper where we correct the proof,

48:50.500 --> 48:53.420
but that required also changing a tiny bit the results.

48:53.420 --> 48:55.980
In particular, the scaling should be respect to 1 over d

48:55.980 --> 48:57.180
plus 1.

48:57.180 --> 49:01.340
The plus 1, if you care, is due to the fact that in RT,

49:01.340 --> 49:04.180
you sample the nodes incrementally.

49:04.180 --> 49:08.060
So you also have to account for time as an additional state

49:08.060 --> 49:08.980
variable.

49:08.980 --> 49:10.220
So that's why you have plus 1.

49:10.220 --> 49:12.980
But anyway, it's just a fine detail.

49:12.980 --> 49:17.900
Similar optimality extension exists for RT.

49:17.900 --> 49:22.020
In order to make RT an optimal sampling-based planner,

49:22.020 --> 49:24.740
optimal means that as the number of samples goes to infinity,

49:24.740 --> 49:26.660
you recover an optimal solution.

49:26.660 --> 49:28.940
You have to do a couple of variations

49:28.940 --> 49:30.900
to the RT algorithm.

49:30.940 --> 49:35.020
Basically, you have to rewire some edges in such a way

49:35.020 --> 49:37.660
that you straighten up your RT.

49:37.660 --> 49:39.660
We're not going to cover the details.

49:39.660 --> 49:42.660
But here, pictorially, I show you the typical output

49:42.660 --> 49:44.420
of an RT algorithm.

49:44.420 --> 49:46.180
You see that there is a lot of zigzag.

49:46.180 --> 49:48.780
And zigzag doesn't really go away

49:48.780 --> 49:50.620
as the number of points goes to infinity.

49:50.620 --> 49:53.620
With RT star, we straighten up the path

49:53.620 --> 49:56.780
by doing some local rewiring that I'm not explaining here.

49:56.820 --> 50:02.580
So we have a much straighter path and hence optimal path.

50:02.580 --> 50:08.620
So the key point here is that optimal variants of PRM and RT

50:08.620 --> 50:09.620
exist.

50:09.620 --> 50:13.220
And they require minor variation to the PRM and the RT

50:13.220 --> 50:13.780
algorithm.

50:13.780 --> 50:15.340
So that's good news.

50:15.340 --> 50:19.420
And there are fairly rigorous mathematical statements

50:19.420 --> 50:24.620
about the optimality in terms of a synthetic convergence.

50:24.620 --> 50:25.700
So observation.

50:25.700 --> 50:29.900
PRM, in general, for given n samples,

50:29.900 --> 50:33.900
is the algorithm that gives you the highest quality solution,

50:33.900 --> 50:37.020
because it carefully connects to the computational space.

50:37.020 --> 50:41.180
RT is a much greener algorithm, going back to that question,

50:41.180 --> 50:42.660
which means that it's faster.

50:42.660 --> 50:45.100
Per sample, you only do one collision check.

50:45.100 --> 50:48.180
PRM, in general, does log n collision checks,

50:48.180 --> 50:51.620
because as we said before, the number of neighbors per sample

50:51.620 --> 50:52.500
is about log n.

50:52.500 --> 50:55.820
And then in PRM, you collision check connections

50:55.820 --> 50:57.380
to every neighbor.

50:57.380 --> 50:59.540
So much faster.

50:59.540 --> 51:01.940
But in general, the quality of the path is poor.

51:01.940 --> 51:04.340
Basically, it traps itself in the initial paths

51:04.340 --> 51:05.380
that it finds.

51:05.380 --> 51:09.620
And then it's unable to get out of those bad paths.

51:09.620 --> 51:13.820
You can offset this behavior partially with the RT star.

51:13.820 --> 51:17.820
But basically, the RT star represents an posterior fix

51:17.820 --> 51:22.380
of what is bad in RT, which prompted Marisi's group

51:22.380 --> 51:26.580
to design another algorithm referred to as FMT star.

51:26.580 --> 51:29.140
The details are in the paper here.

51:29.140 --> 51:32.460
Lucas Johnson was a former PhD student in my group.

51:32.460 --> 51:33.500
He's now at Harvard.

51:33.500 --> 51:40.180
Anyway, so the idea of FMT is to get the best of both worlds

51:40.180 --> 51:41.900
from PRM and RT.

51:41.900 --> 51:44.100
Basically, you tend to have an algorithm that gives you

51:44.100 --> 51:47.100
the solution quality of PRM, but with the same computational

51:47.100 --> 51:49.020
speed of RT.

51:49.020 --> 51:51.700
And the way it works is quite similar to, I think,

51:51.700 --> 51:53.820
what you were saying.

51:53.820 --> 51:56.020
So the way it works is that, assume

51:56.020 --> 51:59.860
that we have built a tree of a path.

51:59.860 --> 52:05.620
And u1 and u2 are two vertices.

52:05.620 --> 52:07.340
So it's a tree.

52:07.340 --> 52:07.940
So it's a tree.

52:07.940 --> 52:08.780
This is one branch.

52:08.780 --> 52:09.940
This is the other branch.

52:09.940 --> 52:12.140
u1 is terminal node for this branch.

52:12.140 --> 52:14.580
u2 terminal node for this branch.

52:14.580 --> 52:20.020
So the idea of FMT is that let's sample a new node, v.

52:20.060 --> 52:25.380
And let's try to attach v to the branch such

52:25.380 --> 52:28.780
that the cost to arrival is minimized,

52:28.780 --> 52:32.460
neglecting possible collisions of the last step.

52:32.460 --> 52:36.340
In particular, we connect v to the node u such

52:36.340 --> 52:40.500
that the cost to arrive to u plus the cost of going from u1

52:40.500 --> 52:47.900
to v, or the cost to go from u2 to v, is minimized.

52:47.900 --> 52:55.900
In doing this, we are lazily neglecting collision avoidance.

52:55.900 --> 52:58.580
We just gradually select the node

52:58.580 --> 53:03.460
that minimize the cost to arrive to v, for example, through u2.

53:03.460 --> 53:06.460
And then, of course, we have to call the collision checker

53:06.460 --> 53:09.780
in order to validate whether this edge is in collision

53:09.780 --> 53:10.740
or not.

53:10.740 --> 53:13.700
In this case, this edge will be in collision.

53:13.700 --> 53:17.660
So you might say, well, then you should consider connecting v

53:17.660 --> 53:18.740
to u1.

53:18.740 --> 53:22.700
The algorithm actually says, OK, I will just keep v altogether,

53:22.700 --> 53:24.180
which means that for each sample,

53:24.180 --> 53:26.740
you only carry out one collision check.

53:26.740 --> 53:30.620
So the key thing that requires a little bit of math to prove

53:30.620 --> 53:37.260
is that since we are lazily collision checking edges,

53:37.260 --> 53:39.340
and sometimes we are suboptimally

53:39.340 --> 53:43.020
connecting due to this laziness, we sometimes

53:43.020 --> 53:45.180
do errors in building this roadmap.

53:45.180 --> 53:47.860
But as the number of points goes to infinity,

53:47.860 --> 53:51.540
such error becomes a vanishingly rare to the point

53:51.540 --> 53:55.380
that it actually will cover the same graph of PRM,

53:55.380 --> 53:58.420
but by only doing one collision check per sample.

53:58.420 --> 54:01.780
So basically, we have, in a way, the computational complexity

54:01.780 --> 54:03.780
of RRT, one collision check per sample,

54:03.780 --> 54:08.180
with the solution quality of PRM.

54:08.180 --> 54:10.500
This is the high-level idea.

54:10.500 --> 54:11.900
There are a few additional details,

54:11.900 --> 54:13.700
but this is basically the bottom line.

54:13.740 --> 54:16.500
And if you will, it's related to the comment

54:16.500 --> 54:19.820
that I think you made or somebody else made.

54:19.820 --> 54:23.660
So why is you connect to your roadmap?

54:23.660 --> 54:26.180
Why you're not more careful?

54:26.180 --> 54:29.900
And instead of just greedingly connect,

54:29.900 --> 54:32.540
you also come from the notion of cost to arrive,

54:32.540 --> 54:36.420
which is very much in style of pathfinding algorithms.

54:36.420 --> 54:44.020
Cool.

54:44.020 --> 54:44.700
All right.

54:44.700 --> 54:48.220
So somebody about sampling-based algorithms.

54:48.220 --> 54:50.300
So the idea of sampling-based algorithms

54:50.300 --> 54:53.780
is to represent obstacles in the workspace,

54:53.780 --> 54:55.940
implicitly in the workspace, and then

54:55.940 --> 54:59.540
to transform the problem of computing

54:59.540 --> 55:04.260
a path of a trajectory into a bunch of subproblems

55:04.260 --> 55:07.300
that are much easier to solve.

55:07.300 --> 55:08.900
And in particular, the subproblems

55:08.900 --> 55:12.340
are the problems whereby we try to connect two samples that

55:12.340 --> 55:15.020
are close to each other.

55:15.020 --> 55:18.900
The two main bottlenecks of sampling-based algorithms

55:18.900 --> 55:21.140
are, one, the collision checker.

55:21.140 --> 55:22.620
We already discussed it.

55:22.620 --> 55:24.580
There are libraries out there that

55:24.580 --> 55:26.100
do collision checking for you.

55:26.100 --> 55:27.740
As a roboticist, typically, you don't

55:27.740 --> 55:30.660
have to develop a new collision checker.

55:30.660 --> 55:34.820
So you have to be aware that collision checking, in general,

55:34.820 --> 55:37.180
is a time-consuming procedure.

55:37.180 --> 55:38.820
The second bottleneck is presented

55:38.820 --> 55:40.940
by nearest neighbor search.

55:40.940 --> 55:45.340
As you were pointing out, we need to search for neighbors.

55:45.340 --> 55:48.300
And even if you use dedicated data structures,

55:48.300 --> 55:51.940
that can be computationally intensive.

55:51.940 --> 55:54.900
But even in that case, there are good solutions

55:54.900 --> 55:58.460
that work well both theoretically and practically.

55:58.460 --> 55:59.380
Yeah?

55:59.700 --> 56:02.500
In your diagrams, I didn't see any path collisions

56:02.500 --> 56:05.380
between two feasible paths.

56:05.380 --> 56:08.420
Are you also checking those collisions, too?

56:08.420 --> 56:10.580
Path collisions between two feasible paths?

56:10.580 --> 56:11.140
Yeah.

56:11.140 --> 56:12.900
So first of all, there is no such a notion,

56:12.900 --> 56:15.660
because these are paths for individual robots.

56:15.660 --> 56:18.260
So the individual robot will take either one or the other.

56:18.260 --> 56:20.820
So it will never crash with itself.

56:20.820 --> 56:25.220
But of course, there are generalizations

56:25.220 --> 56:26.740
of these algorithms to the setting

56:26.860 --> 56:30.620
whereby you have multiple robots, in which case

56:30.620 --> 56:33.140
you have to account for collisions

56:33.140 --> 56:36.740
between trajectories of the different robots.

56:36.740 --> 56:39.260
Dealing with multiple robots is complicated,

56:39.260 --> 56:42.420
because quickly the configuration space

56:42.420 --> 56:44.420
scales up, because now you're adding

56:44.420 --> 56:46.140
the configuration of robot number one

56:46.140 --> 56:48.580
and configuration of robot number two.

56:48.580 --> 56:50.500
But there are techniques out there.

56:50.500 --> 56:52.420
In particular, the book from Steve Navalle

56:52.420 --> 56:53.980
covers some of these techniques.

56:54.020 --> 56:57.820
But again, those are a bit beyond the scope of this class.

56:57.820 --> 56:58.780
Oh, consistently?

56:58.780 --> 57:01.900
Always uniform across the configuration space?

57:01.900 --> 57:02.420
No.

57:02.420 --> 57:04.620
As I was mentioning before, practically

57:04.620 --> 57:06.940
it's done in non-uniform fashion.

57:06.940 --> 57:11.660
Non-uniform could be either driven by a prior information

57:11.660 --> 57:14.060
about a problem, say you want to sample more

57:14.060 --> 57:16.700
closely to your robot, or maybe you

57:16.700 --> 57:19.460
have some big idea about where the obstacle could

57:19.460 --> 57:20.580
be in computational space.

57:20.580 --> 57:22.500
But as I said, the other trend right now

57:22.500 --> 57:25.820
is to use data-driven methods to learn non-uniform sample

57:25.820 --> 57:28.540
distribution.

57:28.540 --> 57:29.220
Very good point.

57:29.220 --> 57:32.500
Yes, typically it's done in a non-uniform way.

57:32.500 --> 57:34.180
But again, these are all variants

57:34.180 --> 57:36.820
that unfortunately we're not going to cover in this class.

57:36.820 --> 57:38.660
But I'm going to go, but I'm giving you

57:38.660 --> 57:43.700
all the references for these variants I discussed at length.

57:43.700 --> 57:45.900
Second step, kino-dynamic.

57:45.900 --> 57:48.420
So you already know what kino-dynamic planning really

57:48.420 --> 57:49.020
entails.

57:49.020 --> 57:50.820
Now we want to find a trajectory that

57:50.820 --> 57:53.540
not only avoids obstacles and possibly

57:53.540 --> 57:56.340
optimizes the cost function, but also respects

57:56.340 --> 58:00.780
the dynamics constraints of your system, which basically means

58:00.780 --> 58:06.820
that at each configuration, your set of available motions

58:06.820 --> 58:12.740
as represented by the velocity vector is constrained.

58:12.740 --> 58:15.700
For example, with a car, you cannot move laterally.

58:15.700 --> 58:19.460
You can only go forward or turn.

58:19.500 --> 58:25.420
So the question is, how do we account for dynamic constraints

58:25.420 --> 58:28.740
into planning by using sampling-based algorithms?

58:28.740 --> 58:32.260
Conceptually, the good news is that this is relatively simple.

58:32.260 --> 58:34.140
The implementation is much more complicated.

58:34.140 --> 58:35.940
But conceptually, it's relatively simple.

58:35.940 --> 58:40.340
And there are two main ways of handling dynamics

58:40.340 --> 58:43.020
within motion planning with sampling-based algorithms.

58:43.020 --> 58:44.820
The first approach is referred to

58:44.820 --> 58:48.420
as a forward propagation-based approach.

58:48.420 --> 58:52.900
The idea is to only rely on the forward propagation

58:52.900 --> 58:54.740
of the dynamics.

58:54.740 --> 58:58.820
Specifically, let's consider again RRT.

58:58.820 --> 59:01.420
The way that the RRT can be implemented

59:01.420 --> 59:05.140
to account for kino-dynamic constraints is relatively simple.

59:05.140 --> 59:11.220
So what you do is to sample a new state randomly,

59:11.220 --> 59:13.580
as you do with RRT.

59:18.580 --> 59:22.740
So this is your tree.

59:22.740 --> 59:25.140
I have a banded path, because now I'm

59:25.140 --> 59:27.220
saying that we are doing kino-dynamic planning.

59:27.220 --> 59:31.540
So the way we go from one sample to the next one

59:31.540 --> 59:32.980
is not necessarily a straight line.

59:32.980 --> 59:35.060
It might be a curved line, because we

59:35.060 --> 59:38.020
have kino-dynamic constraints.

59:38.020 --> 59:43.540
So we sample a new point, which we call x run.

59:43.540 --> 59:45.820
So that's the line here.

59:45.820 --> 59:53.620
We find the closest sample in the tree to x run,

59:53.620 --> 59:56.460
say this one here.

59:56.460 --> 01:00:00.500
And at this point, I select randomly

01:00:00.500 --> 01:00:04.020
the amount of time through which I

01:00:04.020 --> 01:00:07.460
want to forward propagate the motion of the vehicle.

01:00:07.460 --> 01:00:10.940
And the control that I'm going to use in order

01:00:10.940 --> 01:00:14.100
to forward propagate the motion of the vehicle.

01:00:14.100 --> 01:00:17.300
So basically what I do is to solve the differential

01:00:17.300 --> 01:00:22.900
equation x dot equal f of x near.

01:00:22.900 --> 01:00:24.820
Sorry, hold on a second.

01:00:24.820 --> 01:00:29.020
x dot equal f of x and u.

01:00:31.980 --> 01:00:36.060
Let's call this sample x near.

01:00:36.060 --> 01:00:41.100
With the initial condition x0 equal x near.

01:00:41.100 --> 01:00:46.460
With a control input, input u, which

01:00:46.460 --> 01:00:49.940
has been randomly selected.

01:00:57.620 --> 01:00:59.700
So I call this input u bar.

01:00:59.700 --> 01:01:02.900
And then I integrate this differential equation forward

01:01:02.900 --> 01:01:04.980
for t time units.

01:01:04.980 --> 01:01:09.340
So say that this will lead to a path that looks like this.

01:01:09.340 --> 01:01:10.340
And then a stop.

01:01:10.340 --> 01:01:12.020
And this is my new sample.

01:01:15.380 --> 01:01:17.420
So just simulate the dynamics forward.

01:01:17.420 --> 01:01:20.220
But you have certain constraints on the sampling of u, right?

01:01:20.220 --> 01:01:21.940
It wouldn't go backwards?

01:01:21.940 --> 01:01:24.900
No, so you randomly sample u in such a way

01:01:24.900 --> 01:01:27.420
that it belongs to capital set u,

01:01:27.420 --> 01:01:30.340
which is the set of available controls at that state.

01:01:35.620 --> 01:01:37.740
So from intuition standpoint, that's probably the easiest

01:01:37.740 --> 01:01:39.020
thing that you can do.

01:01:39.020 --> 01:01:40.980
You just forward propagate your dynamics

01:01:40.980 --> 01:01:42.980
in order to expand your tree by using

01:01:42.980 --> 01:01:45.980
randomly selected controls and randomly selecting

01:01:45.980 --> 01:01:49.820
horizon to forward expand your dynamics.

01:01:49.820 --> 01:01:52.300
Why would you do that in a selection control?

01:01:52.300 --> 01:01:55.980
It might not renew it in the right direction

01:01:55.980 --> 01:01:57.260
towards the pointer.

01:01:57.260 --> 01:01:58.380
That's right.

01:01:58.380 --> 01:02:03.140
But the idea is that if you do this with one trillion nodes,

01:02:03.140 --> 01:02:05.620
then chances are the sooner or later

01:02:05.620 --> 01:02:07.620
you'll find something that brings you.

01:02:07.620 --> 01:02:09.020
But it's completely uninformed.

01:02:09.020 --> 01:02:10.500
And you are currently right.

01:02:10.500 --> 01:02:14.540
So this is the simplest version of a sampling-based algorithm

01:02:14.540 --> 01:02:16.700
for kinematic problems.

01:02:16.700 --> 01:02:18.980
But as I said, it's extremely wasteful

01:02:18.980 --> 01:02:22.060
because it does not capture any domain

01:02:22.060 --> 01:02:25.620
knowledge about the problem.

01:02:25.620 --> 01:02:28.740
So why would people consider this algorithm?

01:02:28.740 --> 01:02:31.660
It's that in some cases, the structure of the dynamics

01:02:31.660 --> 01:02:34.100
or the structure of the problem is so complicated

01:02:34.100 --> 01:02:35.860
that that's the best you can do.

01:02:35.860 --> 01:02:37.540
So this is your baseline.

01:02:37.540 --> 01:02:40.420
If the problem looks desperately complicated,

01:02:40.420 --> 01:02:43.420
at least you have this algorithm that you can basically always

01:02:43.420 --> 01:02:44.700
implement.

01:02:44.700 --> 01:02:47.740
But in some cases, in many cases, actually,

01:02:47.740 --> 01:02:51.420
and this goes back to your point,

01:02:51.420 --> 01:02:53.060
you can exploit domain knowledge.

01:02:53.060 --> 01:02:56.820
And in particular, what I refer to here as domain knowledge

01:02:56.820 --> 01:02:57.940
is the following.

01:03:01.900 --> 01:03:09.380
Let's assume that now, again, I sample a new node, Xand.

01:03:09.380 --> 01:03:12.700
But we spent two weeks on a trajectory optimization

01:03:12.700 --> 01:03:17.140
that basically is the problem of finding a trajectory that

01:03:17.140 --> 01:03:19.220
connects two points.

01:03:19.220 --> 01:03:23.780
So now, instead of just randomly selecting an input,

01:03:23.780 --> 01:03:26.020
you solve a trajectory optimization problem

01:03:26.020 --> 01:03:28.180
that optimally connects these two samples.

01:03:28.180 --> 01:03:31.420
This provides a much more direct way to get to your goal.

01:03:32.420 --> 01:03:36.100
And a much less wasteful way of sampling controls.

01:03:36.100 --> 01:03:37.500
What is the downside?

01:03:37.500 --> 01:03:41.940
That now, instead of forward propagating the dynamics,

01:03:41.940 --> 01:03:47.860
here, you're solving a trajectory optimization

01:03:47.860 --> 01:03:53.460
problem, which is more computationally intense.

01:03:53.460 --> 01:03:55.260
And that's where we actually bring together

01:03:55.260 --> 01:03:57.860
the field of motion planning and trajectory optimization.

01:03:57.860 --> 01:03:59.420
Kinonomic motion planning, if you

01:03:59.420 --> 01:04:01.300
want to account for domain knowledge,

01:04:01.300 --> 01:04:04.980
amounts to breaking the problem into small trajectory

01:04:04.980 --> 01:04:08.300
optimization problems, solve for those,

01:04:08.300 --> 01:04:12.740
and then a posteriori collision check for this path.

01:04:12.740 --> 01:04:14.300
Does it make sense?

01:04:14.300 --> 01:04:16.580
So in some cases, this trajectory optimization

01:04:16.580 --> 01:04:19.540
can be done analytically or by solving

01:04:19.540 --> 01:04:21.300
simple systems of linear equations,

01:04:21.300 --> 01:04:23.260
like by using differential flatness.

01:04:23.260 --> 01:04:25.700
In some other cases, you need to use

01:04:25.700 --> 01:04:28.580
full-blown optimal control methods,

01:04:28.580 --> 01:04:31.180
such as indirect methods or direct methods,

01:04:31.180 --> 01:04:34.420
best thoughts to the graduate students last week.

01:04:34.420 --> 01:04:37.020
Up to a point where it becomes so complicated,

01:04:37.020 --> 01:04:40.220
then then you go back to RRT with the forward expansion.

01:04:40.220 --> 01:04:44.220
Is it harder to collision check a current line

01:04:44.220 --> 01:04:46.300
generated by motion planning as opposed to a circle?

01:04:46.300 --> 01:04:47.020
No, it's the same.

01:04:47.020 --> 01:04:51.900
Because the way you do it is that you discretize the edge.

01:04:51.900 --> 01:04:53.820
Each point corresponds to a configuration.

01:04:53.820 --> 01:04:55.220
So you again call the very circle.

01:04:55.220 --> 01:04:56.780
So you don't necessarily use the linear structure

01:04:56.780 --> 01:04:57.780
in the previous example?

01:04:57.780 --> 01:04:58.280
No.

01:04:58.280 --> 01:04:58.780
Yeah.

01:04:58.780 --> 01:05:00.660
For collision checking, it's just a black box

01:05:00.660 --> 01:05:02.820
to give and dispose, M and a collision.

01:05:02.820 --> 01:05:04.540
That's exactly the same question that you answer,

01:05:04.540 --> 01:05:06.340
either in this setting or in the other setting.

01:05:09.940 --> 01:05:12.420
Yeah?

01:05:12.420 --> 01:05:15.380
In the trajectory optimization situation,

01:05:15.380 --> 01:05:19.620
what if you randomly sampled a point that was not

01:05:19.620 --> 01:05:21.380
available in your space?

01:05:21.380 --> 01:05:22.540
It is not reachable.

01:05:22.540 --> 01:05:24.540
Yeah, it is not reachable because of the dynamic.

01:05:24.540 --> 01:05:25.580
Yeah, no, great question.

01:05:25.580 --> 01:05:28.980
So one of the nuances of kinematic planning

01:05:28.980 --> 01:05:32.740
is that not only you have to reason about collision

01:05:32.740 --> 01:05:36.020
avoidance, but also about no reachability

01:05:36.020 --> 01:05:39.300
of some of the configurations.

01:05:39.300 --> 01:05:43.820
In particular, and this is related to this point here,

01:05:43.820 --> 01:05:47.220
the two differences between running, say,

01:05:47.220 --> 01:05:52.020
PRM or FMT star with and without kinematic constraints

01:05:52.020 --> 01:05:55.060
is that if you want to account for kinematic constraints,

01:05:55.060 --> 01:05:57.140
the connection between two points

01:05:57.140 --> 01:05:59.420
is not a straight line with actually a solution

01:05:59.420 --> 01:06:02.100
to trajectory optimization problem, number one.

01:06:02.100 --> 01:06:06.740
Number two, when you want to find neighbors,

01:06:06.740 --> 01:06:11.020
you're no longer just finding points within a given radius.

01:06:11.020 --> 01:06:13.700
That is really meaningless for kinematic systems.

01:06:13.700 --> 01:06:17.220
You have to somehow account for the dynamics of your system,

01:06:17.220 --> 01:06:20.500
in particular through the notion of reachable sets.

01:06:20.500 --> 01:06:22.020
What is a reachable set?

01:06:22.020 --> 01:06:24.700
Let's assume that this is the pose of your robot,

01:06:24.700 --> 01:06:28.300
like a unicycle.

01:06:28.300 --> 01:06:32.340
The reachable set is the set of points

01:06:32.340 --> 01:06:36.340
that can be reached within time t.

01:06:36.340 --> 01:06:41.060
So why it has this weird form is that for a car,

01:06:41.060 --> 01:06:44.220
reaching a point in the forward direction

01:06:44.220 --> 01:06:46.620
is easier than reaching it in the lateral direction,

01:06:46.620 --> 01:06:48.780
because to reach a point in the lateral direction,

01:06:48.780 --> 01:06:53.220
you have to go forward, say, and then backward,

01:06:53.220 --> 01:06:55.060
which means that the amount of travel

01:06:55.060 --> 01:06:58.660
that you can do laterally within a given time horizon t

01:06:58.660 --> 01:07:00.260
is less than the amount of travel

01:07:00.260 --> 01:07:04.780
that you can do frontally within a given time t.

01:07:04.780 --> 01:07:07.740
So in this class of algorithms, the way

01:07:07.740 --> 01:07:09.100
that you use nearest neighbors is

01:07:09.100 --> 01:07:13.260
by replacing bolts or spheres with reachable sets.

01:07:13.260 --> 01:07:15.460
And this would account for the fact of whether,

01:07:15.460 --> 01:07:16.740
for example, within a time.

01:07:16.740 --> 01:07:21.980
So for example, let's assume that I'm here,

01:07:21.980 --> 01:07:23.420
and I consider this reachable set.

01:07:26.740 --> 01:07:28.420
The point being here, it means that it

01:07:28.420 --> 01:07:31.180
is beyond what can be reached within time t,

01:07:31.180 --> 01:07:35.220
so it would not be considered for inclusion.

01:07:35.220 --> 01:07:38.860
So usually, instead of considering bolts of radius r,

01:07:38.860 --> 01:07:43.060
you consider reachable sets for a given time horizon t,

01:07:43.060 --> 01:07:44.780
or in general, for a given cost c,

01:07:44.780 --> 01:07:47.180
where the cost could be a combination of time and control

01:07:47.180 --> 01:07:47.980
effort.

01:07:47.980 --> 01:07:49.340
Does it make sense?

01:07:49.340 --> 01:07:52.220
The geometric case, if you think about it,

01:07:52.220 --> 01:07:53.500
is just a special case.

01:07:53.500 --> 01:07:56.980
For the geometric case, what is the reachable set?

01:08:00.420 --> 01:08:03.540
It's just a ball, because for a point with no constraints

01:08:03.540 --> 01:08:07.980
in the dynamics, every direction looks equally feasible.

01:08:07.980 --> 01:08:12.260
So space is isotropic, and the reachable set is a ball.

01:08:12.260 --> 01:08:14.780
You can view kinematic planning as a setting whereby

01:08:14.780 --> 01:08:17.300
you have to squish these balls in order to align them

01:08:17.300 --> 01:08:18.900
with the dynamics.

01:08:18.980 --> 01:08:23.060
So two main differences with respect to geometric planning.

01:08:23.060 --> 01:08:28.580
You have to connect points by using reachable sets that

01:08:28.580 --> 01:08:30.860
might have, sometimes, weird shapes.

01:08:30.860 --> 01:08:34.180
And two, when you connect two samples,

01:08:34.180 --> 01:08:35.860
you cannot just use a straight line,

01:08:35.860 --> 01:08:38.360
but actually, you have to solve the trajectory optimization

01:08:38.360 --> 01:08:39.500
problem.

01:08:39.500 --> 01:08:41.940
If you can do that quickly, for example,

01:08:41.940 --> 01:08:44.500
by exploiting offline computation,

01:08:44.500 --> 01:08:46.460
then this can run very fast.

01:08:46.460 --> 01:08:50.340
For example, for a double integrator model,

01:08:50.340 --> 01:08:52.740
which is simple, but arguably quite representative

01:08:52.740 --> 01:08:55.620
for a number of systems, because you account for momentum.

01:08:55.620 --> 01:09:00.540
In the dynamics, you can solve 3D kinematic planning problems

01:09:00.540 --> 01:09:03.500
in about 50 milliseconds.

01:09:03.500 --> 01:09:07.740
How do you generate the reachable set, or that shape?

01:09:07.740 --> 01:09:08.980
Yes.

01:09:08.980 --> 01:09:11.340
You take my class, it was in the spring.

01:09:11.340 --> 01:09:18.220
So there are well-known methods in optimal control

01:09:18.220 --> 01:09:21.260
to compute those reachable sets.

01:09:21.260 --> 01:09:22.880
So that reachable set, in some cases,

01:09:22.880 --> 01:09:25.940
can be computed analytically.

01:09:25.940 --> 01:09:28.700
Many cases can be computed numerically.

01:09:28.700 --> 01:09:31.460
But unfortunately, this is beyond the scope of this class.

01:09:31.460 --> 01:09:38.060
For this class, if we ask you to consider kinematic planning,

01:09:38.060 --> 01:09:39.460
we will give you that information

01:09:39.460 --> 01:09:40.500
about the reachable set.

01:09:40.540 --> 01:09:42.740
But knowing that for most representative systems,

01:09:42.740 --> 01:09:44.020
that is known.

01:09:44.020 --> 01:09:45.780
So practically, you can do it.

01:09:45.780 --> 01:09:48.940
So for PRM, when we had just a circle, we were doing this.

01:09:48.940 --> 01:09:49.980
Because of geometric.

01:09:49.980 --> 01:09:52.380
PRM, a kinematic, then you will have this.

01:09:52.380 --> 01:09:52.860
Right.

01:09:52.860 --> 01:09:54.660
But in that case, we were picking a radius.

01:09:54.660 --> 01:09:56.700
And that was a parameter we could change.

01:09:56.700 --> 01:09:58.300
So in this case, for the reachable set,

01:09:58.300 --> 01:09:59.500
would you be changing time?

01:09:59.500 --> 01:10:00.000
No.

01:10:00.000 --> 01:10:00.500
Yeah.

01:10:00.500 --> 01:10:03.100
So time, or in general, we talked

01:10:03.100 --> 01:10:05.820
about the cost-bounded reachable sets.

01:10:05.820 --> 01:10:08.900
So the set of the configuration that you can reach,

01:10:08.940 --> 01:10:11.660
within a cost c upper bar.

01:10:11.660 --> 01:10:13.620
Where c could be time.

01:10:13.620 --> 01:10:16.980
In this case, we talked about t limiting the reachable sets.

01:10:16.980 --> 01:10:20.100
Or it could be a combination of time and the control effort.

01:10:20.100 --> 01:10:23.100
So just for clarification, the reachable set

01:10:23.100 --> 01:10:24.940
for all configurations?

01:10:24.940 --> 01:10:28.100
It can be reached within a given cost.

01:10:28.100 --> 01:10:29.460
Subject to the differential cost.

01:10:29.460 --> 01:10:31.220
Subject to the differential cost.

01:10:31.220 --> 01:10:35.940
And here is, again, let's assume that the cost is really time.

01:10:35.940 --> 01:10:37.700
And you have a car.

01:10:37.700 --> 01:10:41.340
And the car has the heading in this direction.

01:10:41.340 --> 01:10:47.060
So you can go farther in the frontal direction.

01:10:47.060 --> 01:10:48.660
And you can travel a smaller distance

01:10:48.660 --> 01:10:50.180
in the lateral direction, because you

01:10:50.180 --> 01:10:52.420
need to concatenate motion primitives.

01:10:52.420 --> 01:10:55.300
You can still do it, because you can go forward and backward,

01:10:55.300 --> 01:10:56.180
and so on.

01:10:56.180 --> 01:10:58.140
But it takes more time.

01:10:58.140 --> 01:11:00.700
That's why, in terms of t limited reachable set,

01:11:00.700 --> 01:11:03.220
the space you can travel leads to the squishing

01:11:03.220 --> 01:11:04.140
of the reachable set.

01:11:04.140 --> 01:11:04.620
Make sense?

01:11:07.860 --> 01:11:09.580
So it's very natural, I think.

01:11:09.580 --> 01:11:11.820
So is the reachable set going to be a static thing?

01:11:11.820 --> 01:11:14.380
Or will you change it based off of the geology

01:11:14.380 --> 01:11:16.860
of the space, or are there any obstacles or something like

01:11:16.860 --> 01:11:17.620
that?

01:11:17.620 --> 01:11:19.780
Yeah, so the idea of planning is try

01:11:19.780 --> 01:11:23.820
to separate and decouple trajectory optimization

01:11:23.820 --> 01:11:25.580
from obstacle avoidance.

01:11:25.580 --> 01:11:28.260
So the reachable sets and the trajectory optimization

01:11:28.260 --> 01:11:32.300
problem are solved without accounting for obstacles.

01:11:32.300 --> 01:11:34.780
And then you account for obstacles up posteriorly

01:11:34.780 --> 01:11:36.060
through collision checking.

01:11:36.060 --> 01:11:38.220
That's really the hallmark of sampling-based planning,

01:11:38.220 --> 01:11:40.260
to try to decouple little things in such a way

01:11:40.260 --> 01:11:42.860
that you gain computational tractability.

01:11:45.500 --> 01:11:49.740
All right, very last topic, and here I'll be quite quick.

01:11:49.740 --> 01:11:52.540
Question, do probabilistic planners,

01:11:52.540 --> 01:11:55.580
like PRM, that stands for probabilistic roadmap,

01:11:55.580 --> 01:11:58.500
or RRT, that stands for collaborative exploring

01:11:58.500 --> 01:12:01.420
random tree, really need to be random,

01:12:01.420 --> 01:12:04.380
meaning relying on a randomized sequence?

01:12:04.380 --> 01:12:06.140
Answer to this question is important,

01:12:06.140 --> 01:12:08.420
because if you're able to actually deal

01:12:08.420 --> 01:12:10.180
with the deterministic sequences,

01:12:10.180 --> 01:12:12.100
you may have a number of advantages.

01:12:12.100 --> 01:12:13.780
It would ease certification process.

01:12:13.780 --> 01:12:15.900
So say that you want to apply this algorithm

01:12:15.900 --> 01:12:20.220
to a flying self-driving car, which is safety critical.

01:12:20.220 --> 01:12:22.580
Having a probabilistic guarantee is not the best

01:12:22.580 --> 01:12:25.100
you can hope for.

01:12:25.100 --> 01:12:27.660
It would allow you to reuse offline computation.

01:12:27.660 --> 01:12:31.660
So instead of sampling new roadmaps every time

01:12:31.660 --> 01:12:33.460
you want to solve a motion planning problem,

01:12:33.460 --> 01:12:36.820
you would use the same roadmap, which

01:12:36.820 --> 01:12:39.580
means that you can pre-store that roadmap,

01:12:39.580 --> 01:12:42.300
pre-compute the, say, two-point malleable problem

01:12:42.300 --> 01:12:44.660
reachable sets, and so on.

01:12:44.660 --> 01:12:46.300
It could also simplify some operations,

01:12:46.300 --> 01:12:48.420
like nearest network searches.

01:12:48.420 --> 01:12:53.260
Turns out that this question is related to the notion of L2

01:12:53.260 --> 01:12:55.100
dispersion.

01:12:55.100 --> 01:12:58.100
The L2 dispersion of a set of points

01:12:58.100 --> 01:13:04.380
is loosely defined as the radius of the largest ball

01:13:04.380 --> 01:13:06.620
that you can fit in that set of points

01:13:06.620 --> 01:13:10.140
without touching none of the points.

01:13:10.140 --> 01:13:12.940
So we say that we have large dispersion.

01:13:12.940 --> 01:13:15.220
If we can fit a ball, this does not

01:13:15.220 --> 01:13:20.620
intersect any of the points with a large radius.

01:13:20.620 --> 01:13:23.940
So given endpoints, the stupid way of sampling

01:13:23.940 --> 01:13:26.740
would be to put all the endpoints here,

01:13:26.740 --> 01:13:29.100
which would lead to a very large dispersion sequence,

01:13:29.100 --> 01:13:31.460
because then the ball that does not

01:13:31.460 --> 01:13:33.740
intersect any of the points would have, say,

01:13:33.740 --> 01:13:35.940
this radius here.

01:13:35.940 --> 01:13:38.300
This would be a decent sequence.

01:13:38.300 --> 01:13:43.780
In particular, formally, the dispersion for a set of points

01:13:43.780 --> 01:13:47.660
is defined as the supremum over all points

01:13:47.660 --> 01:13:51.140
in the space of the minimum distance between that point

01:13:51.140 --> 01:13:53.620
and one of the sample, which basically

01:13:53.620 --> 01:13:57.100
means fitting the largest ball that

01:13:57.100 --> 01:14:00.700
doesn't hit any of the points.

01:14:00.700 --> 01:14:04.420
So you can think that for planning,

01:14:04.420 --> 01:14:13.100
you want to have a set of samples that

01:14:13.100 --> 01:14:15.300
have low dispersion, because that

01:14:15.300 --> 01:14:18.020
ensures that you have good coverage on the environment.

01:14:18.020 --> 01:14:24.300
The natural question is, are sequences

01:14:24.300 --> 01:14:27.420
with the minimum possible dispersion known?

01:14:27.420 --> 01:14:30.140
The question is that, the answer is that, in general, that

01:14:30.140 --> 01:14:31.660
is not known.

01:14:31.660 --> 01:14:33.420
But what is known is that there are

01:14:33.420 --> 01:14:36.100
deterministic sequences whose dispersion

01:14:36.100 --> 01:14:37.940
is asymptotically optimal.

01:14:37.940 --> 01:14:40.540
So as n goes to infinity, at least the scaling

01:14:40.540 --> 01:14:42.620
is the asymptotically optimal scaling.

01:14:42.620 --> 01:14:44.820
And in particular, the scaling goes with n

01:14:44.820 --> 01:14:47.140
to the power of minus 1 over d.

01:14:47.140 --> 01:14:48.380
n is the number of samples.

01:14:48.380 --> 01:14:51.100
It makes sense that the optimal dispersion

01:14:51.100 --> 01:14:55.580
goes to 0 as the number of samples increases.

01:14:55.580 --> 01:14:57.820
But it's not clear how optimally it should go.

01:14:57.820 --> 01:15:00.860
That's the optimal scaling.

01:15:00.860 --> 01:15:06.060
Now, it turns out that if you go back to PRM, in PRM, as I said,

01:15:06.060 --> 01:15:09.540
there are two, basically, degrees of freedom.

01:15:09.540 --> 01:15:15.460
How big is the ball that you use to connect points?

01:15:15.460 --> 01:15:19.500
And then, how do you place the samples

01:15:19.500 --> 01:15:21.620
in the configuration space?

01:15:21.620 --> 01:15:24.580
This, again, the animation of how PRM works.

01:15:24.580 --> 01:15:29.180
So it turns out that if you choose as a sampling

01:15:29.180 --> 01:15:32.660
distribution, the distribution of samples that

01:15:32.660 --> 01:15:35.860
has low dispersion, meaning that the scaling is

01:15:35.860 --> 01:15:37.660
the asymptotically optimal scaling,

01:15:37.660 --> 01:15:40.940
and those sequences are known.

01:15:40.940 --> 01:15:44.260
And if the radius of the ball decreases

01:15:44.260 --> 01:15:47.580
no faster than n to the power of 1 over d,

01:15:47.580 --> 01:15:51.380
then PRM is guaranteed to converge to the optimal path,

01:15:51.380 --> 01:15:53.060
deterministically.

01:15:53.060 --> 01:15:55.020
Which basically means that contrary

01:15:55.020 --> 01:15:57.980
to what people believe for 25 years,

01:15:57.980 --> 01:15:59.860
you don't really need random sampling.

01:15:59.860 --> 01:16:04.060
A deterministic sequence that has low dispersion

01:16:04.060 --> 01:16:08.660
and those sequences are known achieve asymptotic optimality.

01:16:08.660 --> 01:16:10.260
So it was the result that I published

01:16:10.260 --> 01:16:13.220
with one of my students about three or four years ago.

01:16:13.700 --> 01:16:15.460
So it's pretty surprising that still people

01:16:15.460 --> 01:16:17.420
believe that the randomization is really

01:16:17.420 --> 01:16:21.260
the key to the success of RT and PRM, where

01:16:21.260 --> 01:16:22.780
that is absolutely not true.

01:16:22.780 --> 01:16:24.980
That's not really the reason why they are successful.

01:16:24.980 --> 01:16:27.220
They are successful because they cleverly

01:16:27.220 --> 01:16:30.700
exploit the notion of implicit representation of obstacles

01:16:30.700 --> 01:16:32.420
in the configuration space.

01:16:32.420 --> 01:16:35.580
For some reason, people believe that randomization, magically,

01:16:35.580 --> 01:16:38.980
was solving the problem, which is obviously not the case.

01:16:38.980 --> 01:16:40.860
Here is just a bunch of problems where

01:16:40.860 --> 01:16:46.140
I compare the performance of PRM on deterministic sequences.

01:16:46.140 --> 01:16:50.540
And PRM would run on randomized sequences

01:16:50.540 --> 01:16:52.140
and deterministic sequences.

01:16:52.140 --> 01:16:56.740
Basically, less than 100% means that deterministic sequences

01:16:56.740 --> 01:16:57.820
perform better.

01:16:57.820 --> 01:17:00.620
Higher than 100% means that the randomized sequences

01:17:00.620 --> 01:17:01.740
perform better.

01:17:01.740 --> 01:17:04.100
You can see that across the board for planning problem

01:17:04.100 --> 01:17:07.180
in different dimension from 2 to 8,

01:17:07.180 --> 01:17:11.500
with key dynamic chains like for manipulators, aircraft,

01:17:11.500 --> 01:17:14.180
and all sorts of systems, deterministic sampling

01:17:14.180 --> 01:17:16.660
not only has better theoretical properties,

01:17:16.660 --> 01:17:19.820
but actually perform also better in practice.

01:17:19.820 --> 01:17:21.340
On top of it, actually, it turns out

01:17:21.340 --> 01:17:24.460
that we can also provide the convergence rates guarantees,

01:17:24.460 --> 01:17:26.820
meaning how many samples you need in order

01:17:26.820 --> 01:17:30.620
to get to a solution within a certain optimality gap,

01:17:30.620 --> 01:17:34.740
something that was not known until a few years ago.

01:17:34.740 --> 01:17:38.260
So the remaining takeaway is that a synthetic optimality

01:17:38.260 --> 01:17:41.420
can indeed be achieved with the deterministic sequences

01:17:41.420 --> 01:17:43.540
and indeed with a smaller connection of values, which

01:17:43.540 --> 01:17:45.540
means less computation.

01:17:45.540 --> 01:17:48.580
And we can also provide convergence rates.

01:17:48.580 --> 01:17:51.820
And experimentally, deterministic sequences

01:17:51.820 --> 01:17:53.900
appear to provide superior performance.

01:17:53.900 --> 01:17:56.140
So this is something that we published quite recently.

01:17:56.140 --> 01:17:58.380
And it's quite surprising that for so many years,

01:17:58.380 --> 01:18:00.620
people believed that, as I said, randomization

01:18:00.620 --> 01:18:02.660
was the key to the success of these algorithms,

01:18:02.660 --> 01:18:06.140
whereby running the same algorithms on carefully

01:18:06.140 --> 01:18:08.140
constructed deterministic sequences,

01:18:08.140 --> 01:18:10.820
in particular, deterministic sequences with low dispersion,

01:18:10.820 --> 01:18:14.780
gives you better performance that is actually deterministic.

01:18:14.780 --> 01:18:17.340
That opens the door to apply these algorithms

01:18:17.340 --> 01:18:20.580
to synthetic systems, for example.

01:18:20.580 --> 01:18:22.860
All right, so the time is up.

01:18:22.860 --> 01:18:26.180
Next time, well, you had a midterm, sorry.

